BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (  In the dawn of time, the Great Weaver planted the Orchard of Reciprocity. Briar, a humble gard) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (    ener, tended the trees where silver fruit grew. One day, a traveler arrived, weary and empty) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (    -handed. Briar saw that the traveler had nothing, yet the trees flourished only when the fru) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (    it was shared. Briar gave three silver apples to the traveler. Upon receiving them, the trav) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (    eler's touch transformed each apple into a cluster of three golden ones. The traveler, seein) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (    g the sudden abundance, could have kept the entire harvest, but remembered the gardener's in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (    itial trust. He returned a generous portion of the golden clusters back to Briar.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (  "Why share so much?" the traveler asked, bowing low.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (  Briar smiled, continuing to tend the soil. "The orchard does not belong to one. When I share, ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (    the garden grows. When you return, the cycle thrives. If we hoard, the soil turns to dust; i) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (    f we give, the trees reach for the stars.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (  The Orchard of Reciprocity became a legend of balance. It taught all who wandered that trust i) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (    s not a gamble, but a seed. To hold tightly is to starve, but to open one's hands is to invi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (    te a harvest that feeds both the giver and the taker, forever binding them in a dance of mut) Tj ET
