BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (      In the dawn of time, the Great Weaver planted the Orchard of Reciprocity. Briar, a humble ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (        gardener, tended the trees where silver fruit grew. One day, a traveler arrived, weary a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (        nd empty-handed. Briar saw that the traveler had nothing, yet the trees flourished only ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (        when the fruit was shared. Briar gave three silver apples to the traveler. Upon receivin) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (        g them, the traveler's touch transformed each apple into a cluster of three golden ones.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (         The traveler, seeing the sudden abundance, could have kept the entire harvest, but reme) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (        mbered the gardener's initial trust. He returned a generous portion of the golden cluste) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (        rs back to Briar.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (      "Why share so much?" the traveler asked, bowing low.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (      Briar smiled, continuing to tend the soil. "The orchard does not belong to one. When I sha) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (        re, the garden grows. When you return, the cycle thrives. If we hoard, the soil turns to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (         dust; if we give, the trees reach for the stars.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (      The Orchard of Reciprocity became a legend of balance. It taught all who wandered that tru) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (        st is not a gamble, but a seed. To hold tightly is to starve, but to open one's hands is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (         to invite a harvest that feeds both the giver and the taker, forever binding them in a ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (        dance of mutual grace.) Tj ET
