BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (      The harvester, standing at the garden's edge, held the responsibility of the rain. If they) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (         kept the harvest for their own silos, the garden would turn to dust, leaving both with ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (        nothing but barren earth. But if the harvester returned a share to the gardener, the soi) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (        l remained rich and the cycle continued, ensuring a harvest for all seasons. This was no) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (        t a game of extraction, but of stewardship. Each return was a promise kept, a testament ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (        to the fact that abundance grows fastest when it is shared rather than sequestered. Elar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (        a walked the rows, watching the golden stalks rise, knowing that the strength of the gar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (        den lay not in the individual stalks, but in the roots intertwined beneath the surface. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (        To give is to sow; to return is to nurture; together, they feast forever in the harvest ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (        of mutual integrity.) Tj ET
